# `"…".to_set()` is admitted as a constant because its receiver is a literal:
# there is nothing to evaluate in any order, and codegen folds it to the four
# words of the bitfield. A *computed* receiver is an ordinary computation and
# stays refused — otherwise the constant would run `src()` at every use, which
# is exactly what a constant promises not to do.

import { to_set } from builtins;

fn src() -> char[] { "ab" }

let COMPUTED = src().to_set();

fn main() {}
--- errors ---
error: a top-level constant stands for a literal, and "COMPUTED" is given something to evaluate — a constant is substituted into each use, so there is no point at which a computation would run
  --> ./tests/cases/constants/err_computed_to_set.aipl:11:16
   |
11 | let COMPUTED = src().to_set();
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
